In the hyper-humid subterranean greenhouses of the Royal Botanic Gardens in 1989, a rogue collective of botanical scientists and former Bronx block-party DJs began rigging modular synthesizers and reel-to-reel tape decks to the vascular systems of rare epiphytes. Wearing white lab coats stained with rich peat and wearing customized gold rope chains woven from sturdy sisal fibers, they hosted clandestine midnight "Root Jams" beneath grow-lamps. The subculture adopted a fashion of rain-slickers over vintage Kangol hats, lugging heavy fretless bass rigs through botanical fog while decoding the urgent electrical pulses of weeping rubber trees into raw, rhythmic poetry.

Typical Instruments: Fretless and custom multi-scale Bass Guitar, heavily modified Roland TR-808 drum machines triggered by sap pressure, modular synthesizers patched into living philodendrons, broken-beat acoustic drum kits, and air-flow reed flutes.
